    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d16">

                <pb facs="0152" n="44" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg44"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/8023: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-01-10T15:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 10,
                                1927—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 3:35 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>20. Following from American company [<hi rendition="#italic">consul
                    general</hi>,] Shanghai:<anchor n="32" xml:id="d16-f1"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“January 8, 6 p.m. Following sent after consulting Armstrong<anchor
                                n="33" xml:id="d16-f2"/> who is repeating same message to commander
                            in chief:<anchor n="34" xml:id="d16-f3"/></p>
                        <p>In view of recent unexpected turn of events up river which obviously
                            exceeds situation contemplated on point 2,<anchor n="35" xml:id="d16-f4"
                            /> it is recommended that in order to give adequate protection to
                            foreign life and property at Shanghai and to attempt to prevent seizure
                            of Settlement and cannonading foreign-occupied area by mob violence as
                            at Hankow and Kiushu, landing forces Shanghai should be increased to
                            available maximum immediately upon Sun Ch’uan-fang’s<anchor n="36"
                                xml:id="d16-f5"/> initial defeat or indications of any local
                            disorders. Situation Shanghai at present outwardly quiet but highly
                            inflammable upon initial defeat of Sun by Southern forces as is
                            probable.</p>
                        <p>Events in Hankow [and] Kiukiang, give adequate indication of intention of
                            radical element of Nationalist[s] in my telegram of December 30, 3
                                p.m.<anchor n="37" xml:id="d16-f6"/> The Legation appreciates that
                            no distinction as to nationalities will be possible at Shanghai, all
                            foreign lives and interests being equally in danger in the event of mob
                            disturbances.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d16-f1" n="32" xml:id="d16-f1-content">
                <p>Clarence E. Gauss was in charge of consulate general at Shanghai Sept. 8, 1926,
                    to June 7, 1927, in absence of Consul General Edwin S. Cunningham.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d16-f2" n="33" xml:id="d16-f2-content">
                <p>Commander Eldred B. Armstrong, U. S. N., commanding the U. S. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Asheville</hi> of the Asiatic Fleet.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d16-f3" n="34" xml:id="d16-f3-content">
                <p>Admiral Clarence S. Williams, U. S. N., commander in chief, Asiatic Fleet.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d16-f4" n="35" xml:id="d16-f4-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 627, Dec. 19, 1926, from the Minister in China, <ref
                        target="frus1926v01#pg_662"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1926, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 662</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d16-f5" n="36" xml:id="d16-f5-content">
                <p>Military overlord of Provinces of Kiangsu, Kiangsi, Chekiang, Fukien, and Anhwei,
                    and concurrently director general of Shanghai and Woosung Port Administration,
                    allied with Peking Government.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d16-f6" n="37" xml:id="d16-f6-content">
                <p>Not found in Department files.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
